  "body": "**Batch gate on upstream/main a9ec0982** (after the merges of #1147, #1157 and #1143) on macOS arm64 with sccache. Run time 26.7 min. Every suite passed except one test:\n\n`crates/app/tests/mcp_live.rs:410` `a_trickling_client_is_dropped_on_the_idle_schedule`:\n```\nan idle note, not silence - the trickling client was never dropped: Os { code: 54, kind: ConnectionReset, message: \"Connection reset by peer\" }\n```\n- It passed 3/3 in isolation (`cargo test -p camcraft-app --test mcp_live -- a_trickling_client_is_dropped_on_the_idle_schedule --exact`, ~0.5 s each).\n- It failed while the other app test binaries ran in parallel, so it is timing-dependent under load.\n- The read hit a TCP reset (errno 54 on macOS) after the server had already dropped the socket. The test treats that reset as \"never dropped\" instead of as the drop itself.\n- None of #1147/#1157/#1143 touches mcp_live or the idle-drop code.\n\n**Why it matters:** a flaky gate test hides real reds, and a batch tier that fails at random erodes trust in the gate.\n\n**Done means:**\n- The test accepts a connection reset (or EOF) after the idle deadline as the drop, while still refusing a client that stays connected past the schedule. It must not just add retries or longer sleeps.\n- The fixed test passes 20/20 under `--test-threads` load, alongside the other app suites.\n\nLogs: on the gate host, `~/Desktop/camcraft/gate-logs-batch-main/batch-20261113-035938/07-app_*.log` and `mcp_live-rerun.log`.\n",
